    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Let us break down this interaction.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Кристина, ты знаешь одну вещь?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Kristina, do you know one thing?' Chef Viktor combines 'Kristina' with 'thing' in a natural Russian turn of phrase. Then Kristina responds.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anja",
      "t": "Какую именно вещь?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Which exact thing?' Notice how 'what' sets up the clause and leads naturally into 'thing'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я действительно неплохо готовлю.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I truly cook not badly.' This everyday Russian sentence pairs 'I' with 'prepare'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anja",
      "t": "Да, я это прекрасно знаю.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Yes, I know that perfectly well.' Notice how 'yes' sets up the clause and leads naturally into 'know'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Это очень вкусные роллы.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'These are very tasty rolls.' This everyday Russian sentence pairs 'this' with 'sushi roll'. Then Kristina responds.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anja",
      "t": "Ты приготовил их сам?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Did you prepare them yourself?' Kristina uses the informal singular 'you' here with 'myself', as friends and close colleagues do.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Да, специально для тебя.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Yes, specially for you.' Chef Viktor combines 'yes' with 'you' in a natural Russian turn of phrase.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anja",
      "t": "Они выглядят очень аппетитно.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'They look very appetizing.' Notice how 'they' sets up the clause and leads naturally into 'appetizingly'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Хочешь немного попробовать?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Do you want to taste a little?' Chef Viktor combines 'do you want' with 'to taste' in a natural Russian turn of phrase. Then Kristina responds.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anja",
      "t": "Да, с большим удовольствием.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Yes, with great pleasure.' This everyday Russian sentence pairs 'yes' with 'pleasure'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я уверен, тебе очень понравится.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I am sure you will like it very much.' Notice the reflexive ending on the verb for 'will please', which directs the action back onto the subject.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anja",
      "t": "Твоя еда всегда самая лучшая.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Your food is always the best.' Russian omits the present-tense verb 'to be', connecting 'your' directly with 'best'. Now let us listen to the conversation again without interruption.",
